xt for Disease Mentions**\n  - **IF** a disease mention is the head of its own noun phrase, the complement of a preposition or verb, or a clausal subject (that is, it names the condition as an argument), **THEN** label it **SpecificDisease**, even when the sentence is framed around risk, association, family history, or epidemiology, and even when the identical string is labelled Modifier elsewhere in the document.\n  - **IF** a disease mention is an unmarked prenominal noun-adjunct, not governed by a preposition, that directly precedes a head noun it characterizes (such as a population, case set, patient group, study, or gene), **THEN** label it **Modifier**.\n  - **Decision order:** Apply the syntactic-role test first. Use lexical or topical cues only after the role is established.\n  - **Negative constraint:** This rule does **not** apply when (a) the disease string is part of a larger span already governed by the DiseaseClass or CompositeMention definitions, since those definitions take precedence, or (b) the syntactic role is genuinely ambiguous, such as a noun-noun compound that could be either head or adjunct. In case (b), defer to the entity definitions rather than to how often the string is labelled Modifier in the document.", "type": "text"}], "diagnostics": null, "model": "claude-haiku-5-5", "role": "assistant", "stop_details": null, "stop_reason": "end_turn", "stop_sequence": null, "type": "message", "usage": {"cache_creation": {"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0, "ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 0}, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0, "inference_geo": "global", "input_tokens": 1695, "output_tokens": 2967, "output_tokens_details": {"thinking_tokens": 2541}, "service_tier": "batch", "speed": "standard"}}}